    "feedback": "This candidate is valid and inside every contract range. It moves three things at once from the iteration 3 best (724.5): base_speed 1.1 to 1.0 (iteration 5 showed the speed drop alone costs little on the oval, 2.10 s, and gave the slalom a small gain, progress 0.68), mass 0.8 to 0.5 kg, and body_length 0.16 to 0.12 m. wheel_track is back at 0.12, the value that finished the s-bend 20/20 in iteration 3. kp 4.5, ki 0.02, kd 1.0 and the sensor layout (y 0.08, x ±0.05) are unchanged from iteration 3, which kept the slalom failure mode at progress about 0.67. Note that the contract says body_length has no direct effect on dynamics, but the yaw inertia formula includes it, so the size of the inertia gain is uncertain. Mass also changes traction and acceleration, and the three changes are confounded, so read the result with that in mind. Read the race in this order: completion and completion_time on the oval and s-bend (both must still finish under 3 s), then slalom progress and max_error (did the bend exit improve?), then steering_oscillation and line_loss_events (did the lighter, shorter body add jitter or slip?). If the slalom progress rises above about 0.75 with the oval and s-bend still finishing, keep the inertia change and tune from there. If the slalom does not improve but the other tracks still finish, the inertia lever is weak, so return to the iteration 3 body and mass and try a different lever, such as kd 1.2 or ki changes. If steering_oscillation rises above about 0.4, raise kd to about 1.2 before touching kp, since kd 0.8 to 1.0 helped in iterations 2 to 3.",
